numz/ComfyUI-SeedVR2_VideoUpscaler 4490bd1f482e026674543386bb2a4d176da245b9 (Apache-2.0).
https://github.com/numz/ComfyUI-SeedVR2_VideoUpscaler
Changes: namespace relocation; Apex norms replaced with standard LayerNorm/RMSNorm;
window attention runs through the Runtime's `Attention` (windows ordered by length, each run
of equal-length windows one batched call of the kernel the Runtime selects, after ComfyUI's
native SeedVR2); the dense window attention path is removed; checkpoint loading and model movement removed from inference runner;
single Runtime-selected GPU. Fixed upstream conditioning tensors are admitted by the
normalize job from an explicit input Tree into a managed native conditioning component.
Unused upstream device/process-group initialization and legacy state-dict loading
overrides were removed; Runtime owns those actions. Patch imports moved to module scope.
Only the 7B `dit` architecture is included, with standard
normalization (moved from the 3B `dit_v2` package, now deleted); unused upstream
modules (the upscaler wrapper, config/decorator/partition/seed helpers, and the
inflated and non-attention video VAE variants) are omitted.
Colour correction (`seedvr2/color.py`), temporal padding (`seedvr2/frames.py`), input and
latent noise and the batch/overlap/prepend windowing re-implement the numz
ComfyUI-SeedVR2_VideoUpscaler 4490bd1f algorithms (`src/utils/color_fix.py`,
`src/core/generation_phases.py`, `src/core/generation_utils.py`). Histogram matching
(`lab`, `hsv`) runs per frame rather than over the whole batch, so memory follows one frame;
quantile ranks are exact integers.
Inference returns its denoised latents so the adapter records VAE encoding,
denoising and VAE decoding with Runtime's standard telemetry stages. Every random draw (input,
posterior and latent noise) comes from the request's CPU generator; the sampler's progress bar,
the rotary frequencies' cross-call cache and the unused VAE inference helpers are removed.
Without a sequence-parallel group, rank lookup uses the adapter's single-GPU
rank instead of requiring a default PyTorch distributed process group.
VAE encoding and decoding run over spatial tiles from the shape (`split`), declaring each
tile's peak (its causal-convolution caches beside the widest convolution), take frames from
and return them to host memory one slice at a time (encoding takes bytes and scales them on
the device), and clear causal-convolution caches when each pass ends, so device memory
follows the tile and slice instead of the clip.
